          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What screening tool does guidance describe for anxiety, and what does the score change?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found describes the seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire (GAD-7) as the tool most often used for anxiety. It is used both to screen and to track severity. Source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care.\"\n\nThe score sorts severity into bands:\n- 5 or more: mild\n- 10 or more: moderate\n- 15 or more: severe\n\nA score of 10 is the point where further assessment usually follows. Source: the same document.\n\nThe same guidance says preventive-services bodies recommend screening adults under 65 where systems are in place for accurate diagnosis and follow-up. For adults 65 and older, the evidence is judged insufficient to recommend for or against screening. That is a statement about the evidence, not a recommendation against screening.\n\nThe results did not cover how the score is interpreted for a specific person, what happens after a high score, or treatment options beyond general self-management. If you are worried about your own anxiety, a clinician can talk through what a score means for you.\n\nIf you are having thoughts of suicide or self-harm, or you have chest pain, breathlessness or collapse, seek emergency help now. Do not wait for a routine appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
